          description: "A feature-length documentary film about the city of Yakutsk, the Three Sisters, and the search for yesterday. The capital of Russia's Far Eastern Republic of Sakha, a provincial center, is presented here primarily as an architectural curiosity: it is actually a huge prefabricated housing estate in the middle of the taiga, at first glance a rather depressing means of storing human beings, but on closer inspection, it is a lively and rapidly developing city, where the most modern glass and reinforced concrete buildings, prefabricated buildings from the Brezhnev era, and old wooden shacks meet in bizarre combinations, which one of the film's protagonists, Sacha Theater set designer Mikhail Yegorov, called architectural jazz. The impressiveness of these compositions is further enhanced by the fact that Yakutsk is built on permafrost, whose surface sand layers threaten to shift with every summer thaw; therefore, all houses stand on concrete piles that penetrate deep into the never-melting ice. The quintessence of the local disharmonious architectural style is presented in the film by Malupien, a strange castle made of scrap metal, pipes, rods, planks, and various metal parts found in landfills, which its creator, the old geologist Vitaly Mishnin, built instead of a cottage on a plot of land in a cottage colony outside the city to express his belief in the essentially fairy-tale nature of human existence and his relationship to his own home. As a counterpoint to the motifs of Malupien's euphoria as a builder, the film uses motifs from Chekhov's play Three Sisters, which is being staged at the Sacha Theater at the same time. While Chekhov's heroes, Russian provincial intellectuals, wallow in vain dreams, depression, and longing for Moscow, which symbolizes for them the lost paradise of childhood, the creator of the castle made of rubbish in a place called Olšový spas shows that it is only possible to return to childhood in some way by actively creating its image. The director and set designer of Three Sisters also participates in the symbolic raising of the spire, which gives his building an unquestionable sacred meaning, out of pure joy.",
