          description: "A group of men set off on a journey across the frozen surface of the East Siberian Sea and, after a short trek, arrive at an abandoned weather station on Four-Pillar Island. They stay for several days, hunting and talking together. In the snow-covered landscape, people's memories intertwine with those of the old, abandoned buildings. A place where, at first glance, time seems to have stopped long ago, is brought back to life by the men's visit and through the film. When the men leave the station, it remains the same in its snowy immobility, but the viewer has experienced its present and its past. The film is a portrait of the landscape, its past, and the people in it. The film is about a group of hunters from the Nizhnekolymsky District who set out on an expedition to a small archipelago in the East Siberian Sea called the Bear Islands. Today, these islands are an uninhabited protected area with a fairly large population of polar bears. Fifteen years ago, there was a polar hydrometeorological station on one of the islands, Four-Pillar Island, but it was abandoned when the state stopped subsidizing shipping in this part of the Arctic Ocean and it essentially ceased to operate, and when most of the data that the station provided began to be obtained via satellite. All that remains are abandoned, snow-covered buildings, machines, and equipment, places once inhabited by people, now only occasionally visited by curious bears. It is here that the hunters' expedition will stay, disturbing the peace and quiet of this strange ice museum of Soviet times. One of them, an elderly Chukchi man known as Uncle Vanya, lived here for a time sixty years ago, at the end of World War II. His father, a seal hunter, took him with him because the population in the coastal villages was suffering from hunger and disease. The snow-covered buildings of the former polar base, surrounded by endless, perpetually frozen tundra and ice floes, disturb and awaken the old hunter's memory. Uncle Vanya reminisces as he walks through the base, and it is as if the voices of the past suddenly begin to echo... A comparison suggests itself: what was supposed to last forever has disappeared, while a member of a small nation, long predestined for extinction, shows remarkable vitality, a freshness of memory, and an unwillingness to give up on life. The preserved past connected with current events, the endless journey across the frozen plain, the report of a depopulated area after the collapse of socialism, abandonment as a possible harbinger of a new world without humans—all of this can be perceived as the theme of the film.",
